0..74 Comment "// One list with two memory layouts, after Ribbit (Baudon, \"Algebraic Data"
75..147 Comment "// Types for High-Performance Computing\", PhD thesis, ENS de Lyon, 2024,"
148..223 Comment "// Example 3.4): the list of `i32` laid out pointer-tagged, one element per"
224..297 Comment "// pointer, and packed, two elements per pointer with a lone last element"
298..373 Comment "// inline. A layout is a second data type over the list\'s code, admitted by"
374..451 Comment "// an equivalence with the list whose round trips are proved by recursion and"
452..528 Comment "// replayed by the kernel; `ua` makes each equivalence a path between types,"
529..606 Comment "// so a program written once over the list runs on either layout by transport"
607..685 Comment "// and a value moves between layouts along their composite. What is checked is"
686..757 Comment "// the equivalence. The layouts are data, not bytes: each constructor\'s"
758..831 Comment "// comment names the memory type Ribbit gives it, and code emitted from a"
832..906 Comment "// layout stands outside the trust boundary. `equiv` decides no round trip"
907..984 Comment "// over a type that holds itself (#1316), so each equivalence is written out."
986..1059 Comment "/// The list of Ribbit\'s Example 3.1: empty, or an `i32` before the rest."
1060..1063 Keyword "pub"
1064..1068 Keyword "data"
1069..1073 Type "Ints"
